# frozen_string_literal: true

=begin
  uc-leg: diagnostic AND overlay writer for the uber-combat leg-overlay mapper.

  With no argument, this is a read-only diagnostic. Builds the current
  character's live UberCombat::Character and UberCombat::ZoneTable, asks
  UberCombat::ZonePicker for its itinerary (same call as uc-zones.lic), then
  for EACH leg runs it through UberCombat::LegOverlay to print the exact YAML
  that leg's profile overlay would contain, the launch line a person would
  type to run it, and that leg's gap report. Issues no game command and
  writes no file -- rules 1 and 2 in
  custom-scripts/uber-combat/README.md ("Rules that this code obeys"), and
  the same read-only-first discipline uc-zones.lic already used to catch two
  real bugs before anything could act on them. This default behaviour is
  UNCHANGED from the read-only-only version of this script -- it is the
  proven path and it is what a person runs by habit.

  Two additional arguments turn this from diagnostic into actor, always for
  ONE leg at a time, chosen by number from the SAME itinerary the report
  above prints (leg numbers are 1-based, matching the "Leg N" headers the
  default report already prints):

    ;uc-leg write <n>   -- writes leg <n>'s overlay to disk. Does not launch.
    ;uc-leg go <n>       -- writes leg <n>'s overlay, then launches the hunt.

  Both delegate the actual write decision to UberCombat::LegWriter
  (lib/uc_leg_writer.rb), which refuses a leg with ANY gap and refuses to
  overwrite a file it did not itself generate -- see that file's header
  comment for the full safety rationale. This script's own job in both modes
  is unchanged from the read-only path except for the write/launch calls
  themselves: it still derives the same live character, zone table, picker
  and overlay engine set up in #initialize below, and reuses ALL of it -- no
  second copy of that setup exists anywhere in this file.

  Reads two Lich-facing inputs LegOverlay does not read itself (it is pure --
  see lib/uc_leg_overlay.rb's header comment): the uc_weapons / uc_spells
  catalogue off get_settings, and the canonical spell-name list off
  get_data('spells'). Both go through UberCombat::LegSettings first
  (lib/uc_leg_settings.rb), the ONE place that normalises the shape those two
  calls actually hand over -- verified against the real pipeline in
  spec/uc_leg_settings_spec.rb, not assumed. See that file's header comment
  for why get_settings needed its own verification rather than reusing
  uc_zone_table.rb's get_data fix.

  Examples:
    ;uc-leg
    ;uc-leg write 1
    ;uc-leg go 1
=end

# load (not require): edits to the uber-combat lib take effect without
# restarting Lich. Same rationale as uc-zones.lic's load of its own libs.
lib_dir = File.join(SCRIPT_DIR, 'custom', 'lib')
load File.join(lib_dir, 'uc_character.rb')
load File.join(lib_dir, 'uc_zone_table.rb')
load File.join(lib_dir, 'uc_zone_picker.rb')
load File.join(lib_dir, 'uc_leg_overlay.rb')
load File.join(lib_dir, 'uc_leg_settings.rb')
load File.join(lib_dir, 'uc_leg_writer.rb')

require 'yaml'

# Owns only presentation and the Lich-input normalisation LegSettings
# performs. All overlay logic stays in the uber-combat lib; this class prints
# what the lib already computed. No method here issues a game command or
# opens a file for writing.
class UcLegReport
  def initialize
    @character = UberCombat::Character.new
    @zone_table = UberCombat::ZoneTable.from_game_data

    # get_settings with no flex suffix: always ['setup'] only
    # (setup_files.rb:77, "['setup', character_suffixes]"). uc_weapons and
    # uc_spells live in <Character>-setup.yaml, never in a flex file, so
    # this is the plain character setup, not a flex-merged settings object
    # -- deliberately NOT get_settings(['uc']), which would additionally
    # merge in a stale <Character>-uc.yaml left over from a previous run of
    # the (not yet written) disk-writing component, if one exists on disk.
    settings = get_settings
    warn_about_legacy_keys(settings)
    uc_weapons = UberCombat::LegSettings.weapons(settings)
    uc_spells = UberCombat::LegSettings.spells(settings)
    known_spell_names = UberCombat::LegSettings.known_spell_names(get_data('spells'))

    # The picker is built AFTER get_settings on purpose. It needs the account
    # tier, and that is a profile value, so there is nothing to construct it
    # with until the settings are in hand. Leaving the argument off does not
    # fail: it defaults to false and silently gates a premium character out of
    # the zones they paid for (uc_zone_picker.rb#initialize).
    warn_about_max_skills(settings)
    @picker = UberCombat::ZonePicker.new(
      @character, @zone_table,
      UberCombat::LegSettings.premium(settings),
      province: UberCombat::LegSettings.in_province_only(settings),
      max_skills_per_leg: UberCombat::LegSettings.max_skills_per_leg(settings)
    )

    @overlay_engine = UberCombat::LegOverlay.new(@character, uc_weapons,
                                                 uc_spells: uc_spells,
                                                 known_spell_names: known_spell_names)
  end

  def run
    DRC.message(format('=== uc-leg: %s, %d zones loaded ===', XMLData.name, @zone_table.zones.size))

    itinerary = @picker.build_itinerary
    report_itinerary(itinerary)

    all_gaps = []
    itinerary.legs.each_with_index do |leg, index|
      all_gaps.concat(report_leg(leg, index))
    end

    report_target_path
    report_gap_summary(all_gaps)
  end

  # ;uc-leg write <n> and ;uc-leg go <n> both land here. leg_arg is whatever
  # string script.vars handed the argument parser -- validated below before
  # it is trusted as a leg index, never used until it is. Resolves the SAME
  # itinerary #run itself would print (built fresh, per Character/ZoneTable's
  # own "always live, never a snapshot" rule -- uc_character.rb:6-8), so a
  # leg written here always matches what a person last saw the default
  # report say about that leg number.
  #
  # launch: true starts the hunt via start_script -- see #launch_hunt for why
  # that call, specifically, and why it only ever runs after a successful
  # write.
  def write_leg(leg_arg, launch: false)
    itinerary = @picker.build_itinerary
    leg_number = parse_leg_number(leg_arg, itinerary.legs.size)
    return unless leg_number

    leg = itinerary.legs[leg_number - 1]
    overlay = @overlay_engine.build(leg)

    DRC.message(format('-- Leg %d overlay (zone=%s) --', leg_number, leg[:zone_key]))
    report_gaps(overlay.gaps)

    # Rule 5 (the brief): print the full path and the outcome on EVERY
    # attempt, success or refusal -- LegWriter.write never writes, and this
    # method never launches, silently.
    result = UberCombat::LegWriter.write(overlay, target_path)
    report_write_result(result)

    launch_hunt if launch && result.written
  end

  private

  # A present-but-unusable max_skills_per_leg falls back to the default, which
  # is the safe direction but is also invisible. Say so once, or a person who
  # wrote `max_skills_per_leg: "3"` sees the default itinerary and concludes
  # the setting does nothing.
  def warn_about_max_skills(settings)
    return unless UberCombat::LegSettings.bad_max_skills_per_leg?(settings)

    DRC.message('uc: max_skills_per_leg must be a positive whole number. ' \
                'Ignoring it and using the default.')
  end

  # Every uber-combat setting now lives under one uc_settings: key. A profile
  # left half-migrated fails SILENTLY and its symptom looks like a real
  # answer: uc_settings is absent, so the weapons catalogue reads as empty and
  # EVERY leg skill reports :no_weapon_entry -- which is indistinguishable
  # from "this character has no catalogue yet". One line naming the old key
  # turns that confusing gap report into an obvious cause.
  def warn_about_legacy_keys(settings)
    legacy = UberCombat::LegSettings.legacy_keys(settings)
    return if legacy.empty?

    DRC.message(format('uc-leg: WARNING -- %s still set at the top level of your setup file.',
                       legacy.join(' and ')))
    DRC.message('  These moved under uc_settings: (weapons: and spells:). The old keys are ignored.')
  end

  # leg_count: itinerary.legs.size for the itinerary just built -- always
  # freshly computed by the caller, never cached, so this bound can never go
  # stale between the report and the write.
  #
  # Returns the validated 1-based leg number, or nil (after printing exactly
  # why) if leg_arg is missing, not a plain positive integer, or out of
  # range. nil is the caller's signal to stop -- #write_leg never reaches
  # LegWriter with an unvalidated index.
  def parse_leg_number(leg_arg, leg_count)
    if leg_arg.nil?
      DRC.message('uc-leg: missing leg number. Usage: ;uc-leg write <n>  or  ;uc-leg go <n>')
      return nil
    end

    unless leg_arg =~ /\A[1-9][0-9]*\z/
      DRC.message("uc-leg: leg number must be a positive integer, got #{leg_arg.inspect}")
      return nil
    end

    leg_number = leg_arg.to_i
    unless (1..leg_count).cover?(leg_number)
      DRC.message("uc-leg: leg #{leg_number} does not exist -- the current itinerary has #{leg_count} leg(s)")
      return nil
    end

    leg_number
  end

  # Starts the hunt the same way hunting-buddy's own flex-file convention
  # already reads it (contract Q1/Q9: 'uc' is the flex suffix, so
  # get_settings(['uc']) inside hunting-buddy.lic reads exactly the file
  # LegWriter just wrote). start_script wraps Script.start
  # (lich-5/lib/global_defs.rb:19-24, "Script.start(script_name,
  # cli_vars.join(' '), flags)") and returns immediately -- it does NOT block
  # until hunting-buddy finishes, matching how dr-scripts itself launches
  # hunting-buddy from coordinator.lic:253 ("start_script('hunting-buddy',
  # [file])") and how hunting-buddy.lic:568 launches combat-trainer the same
  # non-blocking way. This script exits right after issuing the call; the
  # hunt keeps running as its own script.
  def launch_hunt
    DRC.message('-- Launching hunt --')
    DRC.message('  start_script(\'hunting-buddy\', [\'uc\'])  (non-blocking; this script exits now)', false)
    start_script('hunting-buddy', ['uc'])
  end

  # Prints the path and the outcome for a write attempt -- the "never write
  # silently" rule applies to refusals just as much as to a success; a
  # refusal a person doesn't see is a leg that silently never advances.
  def report_write_result(result)
    DRC.message('-- Overlay write --')
    DRC.message("  path: #{result.path}", false)
    if result.written
      DRC.message('  OUTCOME: WRITTEN', false)
      return
    end

    DRC.message("  OUTCOME: REFUSED (#{result.reason})", false)
    case result.reason
    when :gaps
      DRC.message('  Fix the gap(s) reported above, then retry.', false)
    when :foreign_file
      DRC.message('  A file already exists at this path and was NOT generated by uc-leg.', false)
      DRC.message('  It carries no uc-leg marker line, so it is left untouched.', false)
      DRC.message('  Remove or rename it by hand before uc-leg can manage this path.', false)
    end
  end

  # Verbatim from uc-zones.lic's own report_itinerary -- same itinerary, same
  # presentation, so the two diagnostics never disagree about what the
  # picker returned.
  def report_itinerary(itinerary)
    DRC.message('-- Itinerary (build_itinerary) --')
    if itinerary.legs.empty?
      DRC.message('  (no legs)', false)
      return
    end

    itinerary.legs.each_with_index do |leg, index|
      stance = leg[:stance]
      DRC.message(format('  Leg %d: %s', index + 1, leg[:skills].join(', ')), false)
      DRC.message(format('    zone=%-24s stance=%-12s defences=%s',
                         leg[:zone_key], stance[:policy],
                         @character.stance_order(stance[:policy]).join(' > ')), false)
    end

    report_unresolved_premium(itinerary.unresolved_premium)
  end

  # The premium gate falls OPEN on a zone nobody has annotated yet, because
  # closing it would empty a 363-zone table while the harvest is incomplete
  # (uc_zone_picker.rb#premium_locked?). That choice is only defensible while
  # every unknown an actual leg leans on gets named here. Print nothing and a
  # non-premium character walks to a guard, gets turned back, and reads no
  # reason for it anywhere.
  def report_unresolved_premium(records)
    return if records.nil? || records.empty?

    DRC.message('  -- Premium status unknown for these leg zones --', false)
    records.each do |record|
      DRC.message(format('    %-24s skills=%s', record[:zone_key],
                         record[:detail][:skills].join(', ')), false)
    end
    DRC.message('    If a hunt cannot travel to one of these, it is premium only.', false)
  end

  # Returns this leg's gaps, tagged with the leg number, so the closing
  # summary can name which leg each gap belongs to without re-deriving it.
  def report_leg(leg, index)
    overlay = @overlay_engine.build(leg)

    DRC.message(format('-- Leg %d overlay (zone=%s) --', index + 1, leg[:zone_key]))
    print_yaml(overlay.settings)
    DRC.message('  Launch: ;hunting-buddy uc', false)
    report_gaps(overlay.gaps)

    overlay.gaps.map { |gap| gap.merge(leg: index + 1) }
  end

  # settings.to_yaml is the exact serialisation the (not yet written) disk
  # writer would emit -- Overlay#settings is a plain Hash, and Ruby's own
  # YAML dump is the only faithful way to show what "as it would be written"
  # means for a Hash mixing Symbol and String keys (contract Q1). DRC.message
  # takes one line at a time (it has no multi-line contract of its own,
  # unlike Lich::Messaging.mono), so the block splits the dump itself rather
  # than handing it a single string with embedded newlines.
  def print_yaml(settings)
    settings.to_yaml.each_line { |line| DRC.message(line.chomp, false) }
    # YAML's document-end marker, and it earns its place. Without it the next
    # line printed sits directly beneath the dump at the same indent and reads
    # as one more YAML key -- the launch line rendered as "  Launch:" nested
    # under "stances:". This output exists to be read against, and pasted into,
    # a real profile file, so the end of the document must be unmistakable.
    DRC.message('...', false)
  end

  def report_gaps(gaps)
    if gaps.empty?
      DRC.message('  -- Gaps: none --', false)
      return
    end

    DRC.message('  -- Gaps: DO NOT launch this leg --', false)
    gaps.each do |gap|
      DRC.message(format('    %-16s reason=%-20s %s', gap[:skill], gap[:reason], gap[:detail]), false)
    end
  end

  # The one absolute path this script ever targets, for both the read-only
  # report and an actual write. Derived at runtime through SetupFiles' own
  # private resolution (scripts_profiles_path, to_character_filename) rather
  # than hardcoded, so this line never drifts from contract Q9's actual
  # answer if the game-instance-specific branch there
  # (setup_files.rb:157-160) ever starts applying on this install.
  # $setupfiles is the process-wide global instance every get_settings /
  # get_data call already goes through (global_defs.rb:68).
  #
  # This is the ONLY place a target path is computed anywhere in this
  # script or in UberCombat::LegWriter -- the writer takes whatever path it
  # is given and neither hardcodes nor re-derives one itself (see that
  # file's header comment). There is deliberately no argument anywhere in
  # this script's CLI parsing that can override or extend this path: the
  # only value ;uc-leg write/go ever accepts from a person is a leg NUMBER
  # (#parse_leg_number), never a path or a filename fragment.
  def target_path
    File.join($setupfiles.send(:scripts_profiles_path), $setupfiles.send(:to_character_filename, 'uc'))
  end

  def report_target_path
    DRC.message('-- Overlay file target (NOT written by this script) --')
    DRC.message("  #{target_path}", false)
  end

  def report_gap_summary(all_gaps)
    DRC.message('=== GAP SUMMARY ===')
    if all_gaps.empty?
      DRC.message('  No gaps. Every leg overlay above is complete.', false)
      return
    end

    DRC.message(format('  %d GAP(S) FOUND -- DO NOT launch any leg listed below with ;hunting-buddy uc', all_gaps.size))
    all_gaps.each do |gap|
      DRC.message(format('    Leg %-3d %-16s reason=%-20s %s',
                         gap[:leg], gap[:skill], gap[:reason], gap[:detail]), false)
    end
  end
end

# script is bound to Script.current by Lich's own script-launch eval
# (lich-5/lib/common/script.rb:176, "eval('script = Script.current',
# script_binding, script.name)") -- the same mechanism every dr-scripts CLI
# parser below relies on, not a name this file invents. vars[0] is the whole
# joined argument string; vars[1], vars[2], ... are the individual
# whitespace-split tokens (lich-5/lib/common/script.rb:3098-3099,
# "cli_vars.each_index { |idx| @vars[idx + 1] = cli_vars[idx] }"). This is
# the plain positional-arg convention used throughout dr-scripts/scripts for
# a script that is not a flex-settings consumer itself (script.vars[1] /
# script.vars[2] checks: scripts/scripts/autostart.lic:379-425,
# scripts/scripts/alchemy.lic:2687-2696) -- uc-zones.lic takes no arguments
# at all, so there is no sibling convention in THIS project to match instead.
#
# No argument (script.vars.empty?) is the untouched read-only path -- the
# case that must never change. 'write' and 'go' are matched
# case-insensitively, the same style as the autostart.lic citation above.
mode = script.vars[1]

case mode
when nil
  UcLegReport.new.run
when /\Awrite\z/i
  UcLegReport.new.write_leg(script.vars[2])
when /\Ago\z/i
  UcLegReport.new.write_leg(script.vars[2], launch: true)
else
  DRC.message("uc-leg: unknown argument '#{mode}'. Usage: ;uc-leg | ;uc-leg write <n> | ;uc-leg go <n>")
end
